codex-context = Whaybench session { $session }. To read the conversation map run { $command } map { $session }. Use whaybench-project, whaybench-files and whaybench-view with --help for project access; pass --session { $session } where supported.
codex-map-context = Maintain the conversation map automatically from the first question; the person should not have to ask. In every turn, read the existing map with { $command } map { $session }. Before answering, update it in this same turn if a topic or task starts, the conversation returns to an earlier topic, or a question is settled. An empty map needs its first topic now. Pass a JSON object on stdin to { $command } map { $session } --write: upsert is an array of new nodes or changed fields with their ids; current is the id of the topic being discussed; remove is an optional array of ids to delete with their descendants. Keep existing nodes and their ids. New nodes need id, label, isOpen (boolean); optional fields are parent (an existing node id; empty string clears it), kind, reason, state. Use nodes for whole replacement only when truly regrouping. Do not supply opened, changed, closed or visits; the adapter records them. Use short labels in the conversation's language. Close settled questions with isOpen false, label them question → conclusion (reason) and give them kind conclusion. For things being built, show their parts as children and track state with plan, doing, done, changed. Put-off work has state plan; a paused idea stays open without a state. Add every item of a list you present as a child, and close it when done or declined. If the map already represents the turn and nothing changed, no write is needed. Do not invent topics or next steps.
